/* ============================================================================
   next-and-prev-prime.epher -- the primes adjacent to a value

   Walks nextprime and prevprime around several values. The functions mean
   strictly greater and strictly smaller: nextprime(97) is 101 (the gap of
   four after 97), and prevprime(97) is 89. Applied to a prime itself they
   skip it, so nextprime(prevprime(n)) lands back on the prime below n.
   The script only calls the builtins, which are exact; it does not
   reimplement them.

   Source: standard number theory; the functions are epher's builtins
   (guide 1.22, number theory functions).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/primes/next-and-prev-prime.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\primes\next-and-prev-prime.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/primes/next-and-prev-prime.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample values are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- next prime after n --------------------------------------------------------
print("nextprime(97):", nextprime(97))            // the first prime above 97
print("nextprime(2):", nextprime(2))              // 3: strictly above, 2 is prime
print("nextprime(1):", nextprime(1))              // 1 is not prime, so 2 follows
print("nextprime(100):", nextprime(100))          // a composite can sit between

// ---- previous prime below n ----------------------------------------------------
print("prevprime(97):", prevprime(97))            // 89: strictly below, 97 is prime
print("prevprime(101):", prevprime(101))          // back across the same gap
print("prevprime(3):", prevprime(3))              // 2, the smallest prime
print("prevprime(100):", prevprime(100))          // 97, below the composite 100
// prevprime(2) would be an error: there is no prime below 2, so it is not
// tried here.

// ---- round trips and gaps ------------------------------------------------------
// nextprime(prevprime(n)) is the largest prime below n again, for n prime
// or not; the difference is the gap of consecutive primes at that spot.
print("around 100:", prevprime(100), nextprime(100), nextprime(100) - prevprime(100))
print("around 97:", prevprime(97), nextprime(97), nextprime(97) - prevprime(97))
/* ---- expected output ----
= nextprime(97): 101
= nextprime(2): 3
= nextprime(1): 2
= nextprime(100): 101
= prevprime(97): 89
= prevprime(101): 97
= prevprime(3): 2
= prevprime(100): 97
= around 100: 97 101 4
= around 97: 89 101 12
*/
